module sysl.posix.time
@requires(posix, libc)

import sysl.time.{Instant, Duration, micros}

/** Reading the two clocks the host keeps, which is the one thing `sysl.time` deliberately cannot do.
 *
 * **This is a module of its own so that `sysl.time` is not.** The calendar and the arithmetic ask for
 * no capability and run on a bare machine; `clock_gettime(2)` is asking an operating system for
 * something, and a capability requirement is module-wide -- so a `now()` written next to `Instant`
 * would cost the whole of the date-time library its freestanding reach for one function. `sysl.rand`
 * and `sysl.posix.rand` are split for exactly this reason, and that file's header makes the argument
 * at length.
 *
 * ## The two clocks are different clocks, and the return types say so
 *
 * `now` answers an `Instant`: a point on the timeline, counted from 1970, comparable with one taken
 * on another machine. It is the clock somebody can *set*, so it can move backwards, and subtracting
 * two readings of it to time something is a measurement that a passing `ntpd` can make negative.
 *
 * `monotonic` answers a `Duration`, not an `Instant`, and that is the whole of the distinction: it is
 * counted from an origin nobody specifies -- boot, usually -- so a single reading means nothing and
 * only the difference of two does. It cannot be set and cannot go backwards, which is what makes it
 * the one to measure with. Giving it a type that no calendar function accepts is what stops it being
 * mistaken for a timestamp.
 *
 * ## What a freestanding target does instead
 *
 * **Nothing here, and deliberately nothing shared with it.** A board's clock is a board's decision:
 * two boards carrying the same chip -- so the same *target* -- can count time from a different RTC,
 * which is precisely the case a `#if` on the target cannot express. So an embedded environment
 * supplies a module of its own with these two function names, in its own package, and a program picks
 * its clock by which one it imports.
 *
 * **That used to give up a program compiling unchanged against both, and it no longer does.** The
 * shape this paragraph described as unbuilt -- a symbol declared in capability-free `sysl.time` that
 * host and board alike link an implementation of -- is built: `sysl.time.now` and
 * `sysl.time.monotonic` are it, `library/sysl/time/clock.sysl` carries the argument, and the two
 * `@export`s at the foot of this file are what answer it on a host. The names above are unchanged, as
 * that paragraph promised, so nothing that imported this module has moved.
 *
 * **Both spellings are right and neither is deprecated.** A program that is only ever going to run on
 * a host imports this module and calls `now` directly, which needs no seam and no linker cooperation.
 * A program that wants to move imports `sysl.time` instead and lets whatever it is linked against
 * decide.
 */

// **Nothing here transcribes a C structure, and nothing here names a clock by number.** Both of
// those were done in sysl until the library could carry C, and both were the same mistake in
// different clothes: `struct timespec` is caller-allocated, and `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` is a `#define`
// whose value is 1 on Linux, 6 on macOS and 4 on the BSDs. `reference/ffi.md § A library may carry
// C` names a layout and a macro as the two things only C can reach, and this file needed both.
//
// The shim beside this one answers with the finished number, so neither crosses. What that retires:
//
//   - **the mirror.** `[2]long` was `struct timespec` on every target that could reach this module,
//     which was true and unverifiable -- nothing in a program could check it, and `sizeof` would have
//     reported what sysl laid out rather than what C did. It was also the one place in `library/sysl`
//     that transcribed a structure at all, which `sysl.posix.tty` used to cite as the exception to
//     its own rule. There is now no exception.
//   - **the id table**, and with it the `#else` that declared a symbol nobody defines. That existed
//     because the library is compiled whole for every target, so a branch declaring nothing would
//     have failed in the compiler on all of them rather than at the link on the one that mattered.
//     A per-OS directory says the same thing without the trick: an operating system with no folder
//     here has no shim, and a program that asks the time fails at the link naming the symbol it could
//     not find. Same behaviour, and now it is the general mechanism rather than a hand-written one.
//   - **the 32-bit worry.** `x86-linux` was the target to watch, because a 32-bit glibc `time_t` is
//     not a `long` unless `_TIME_BITS=64` says so, and two `long`s is then not that structure. The
//     shim reads the header, so whoever measures i386's calling convention inherits nothing here.
@blocks
private[sysl] extern "sysl_posix_time_nanosleep" c_nanosleep(nanos: long) -> long
private[sysl] extern "sysl_posix_time_realtime_us" c_realtime_us() -> long
private[sysl] extern "sysl_posix_time_monotonic_us" c_monotonic_us() -> long

/** The wall clock: where the host thinks it is on the timeline.
 *
 * **Two readings of this may differ by anything at all**, including a negative amount, because it is
 * the clock a person and an `ntpd` are both allowed to set. Use it to stamp something, and use
 * `monotonic` to measure something.
 */
now() -> Instant = Instant(c_realtime_us())

/** A monotonic counter, as a length of time from an origin this deliberately does not name.
 *
 * One reading answers nothing. **Two readings subtracted answer how long something took**, and that
 * is the only question it can be asked:
 *
 * val t0 = monotonic()
 * work()
 * print(s"took ${(monotonic() - t0).us}us")
 *
 * It never goes backwards and nothing can set it, which is the difference from `now` and the reason
 * a measurement wants this one.
 */
monotonic() -> Duration = micros(c_monotonic_us())

// ## Waiting
//
// **`sysl.time.sleep` is the wait, and this module answers its seam on a host** (`supply_sleep_us` at
// the foot of the file), so a program that waits through `sysl.time` runs unchanged on a board whose
// package answers the same seam over its timer. `sleep` here is that same function under the name
// this module has always had for it; `nanosleep` below stays this module's own, being the one wait a
// `Duration` cannot name.

/** Wait for `d`, however many attempts that takes -- `sysl.time.sleep`, which waits through the seam
 * this module answers on a host.
 *
 * **A signal cuts a wait short, and this one carries on.** `nanosleep(2)` returns early with `EINTR`
 * and the time still owed, so a wait written as one call is a wait that is quietly shorter than it
 * asked for whenever anything arrives -- which is exactly when a test that depends on it starts
 * looking flaky. The seam answers the remainder and `sysl.time.sleep` asks again for it until nothing
 * is left.
 *
 * **It answers nothing, because there is nothing left to say**: it returns when the time has passed.
 * A caller that wants to *know* it was interrupted wants `nanosleep` below.
 *
 *     sleep(50.ms)
 *
 * A duration of zero or less returns at once rather than yielding, which is not the same thing --
 * `sleep(0.s)` is not a way to offer the processor to something else.
 */
@blocks
sleep(d: Duration) = sysl.time.sleep(d)

/** Wait for `nanos` nanoseconds, and answer how many were still owed when it stopped.
 *
 * **Zero means the whole time passed.** Anything else is what a signal interrupted, and handing it
 * back rather than swallowing it is the difference from `sleep`: a program with a handler installed
 * can tell "the wait finished" from "something happened", which one call to `sleep` cannot.
 *
 *     var left = nanosleep(2_000_000)
 *
 *     while left > 0 && !cancelled()
 *         left = nanosleep(left)
 *
 * **Nanoseconds, where `sleep` takes a `Duration`, and that is what this is for.** A `Duration`
 * counts microseconds, so it cannot name a wait shorter than one -- and `nanosleep(2)` can. Reach
 * for `sleep` unless you need the resolution or the remainder.
 */
@blocks
nanosleep(nanos: long) -> long = c_nanosleep(nanos)

// ## Answering `sysl.time`'s seam
//
// `sysl.time` declares `sysl_wall_us`, `sysl_monotonic_us` and `sysl_sleep_us` and defines none of
// them, so a program that writes the portable `sysl.time.now()` or `sysl.time.sleep` needs somebody
// to. On a host that is this module, and the three functions below are the whole of it
// (`library/sysl/time/clock.sysl` carries the argument).
//
// **Nothing here changes for a caller.** `now` and `monotonic` above are the same functions they
// were, imported and called the same way, and a host program that names this module directly needs
// none of this. What the seam adds is the *other* spelling, for a program that wants to move.
//
// **There is no `#if posix` around these and none is needed, which is worth stating because the
// instinct to add one is right about the danger and wrong about who already handles it.** The danger
// is real: a `define` costs what a `declare` does not, so an unconditional body calling
// `clock_gettime` reaching a freestanding link would fail naming a symbol that target has never had.
//
// What answers it is this file's own header. `@requires(posix, libc)` is a property of the whole
// module, and a supplier is taken only from a module whose clause the target satisfies -- so on a
// machine without `posix` or a C library these two are never reached, and a `#if` would be a second
// statement of a claim the header already makes. (An earlier version of this paragraph offered a byte-identical freestanding archive
// as the proof. That archive was missing the supplier on *every* target, POSIX ones included,
// because nothing put a library supplier in the program at all; `LibrarySupplierCliTests` now holds
// both halves -- defined on a POSIX target, left declared on a freestanding one.)
//
// So a freestanding program that asks the time and links no supplier fails at the link naming
// `sysl_wall_us`, which is the diagnostic it should get: the board is what has a clock to offer, and
// this module is not on that board.

/** Answers `sysl.time`'s wall-clock seam. **Call `now` instead** -- this exists to be linked, not to
 * be called. It is public although an `@export` may be `private` (`reference/ffi.md § A private
 * export`): a private export's symbol is hidden, a hidden definition keeps a Mach-O artifact to one
 * codegen unit, and the standard module's artifact is the one every build pays that cost in.
 *
 * The name says what it is for rather than what it reads, so that it does not compete with anything
 * a program would reach for -- and a package answering the same seam on a board writes the same two
 * lines with its own chip behind them.
 */
@export("sysl_wall_us")
supply_wall_us() -> long = c_realtime_us()

/** Answers `sysl.time`'s monotonic seam. **Call `monotonic` instead** -- this exists to be linked
 * rather than called, and `supply_wall_us` beside it says why it is public.
 */
@export("sysl_monotonic_us")
supply_monotonic_us() -> long = c_monotonic_us()

/** Answers `sysl.time`'s sleep seam: one `nanosleep(2)` of `us` microseconds, answering the
 * microseconds still owed when it stopped. **Call `sleep` instead** -- this exists to be linked.
 *
 * A remainder is rounded up to the next microsecond, so an interrupted wait is never reported as
 * finished while a fraction of one is still owed.
 */
@export("sysl_sleep_us")
supply_sleep_us(us: long) -> long
    val left = c_nanosleep(us * 1000)

    (left + 999) / 1000
